      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess the effectiveness of high-volume image-guided injection in the middle term in patients with recalcitrant patellar tendinopathy."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Case series study; Level of evidence, 4."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "All tertiary referrals, public, and private healthcare."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS",
          "content": "Forty-four patients (41 men and 3 women) with diagnosis of recalcitrant patellar tendinopathy were included."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "Tendon injection of a mixture of 10 mL of 0.5% bupivacaine hydrochloride, 62\u2009500 international units of aprotinin, and 40 mL of normal saline solution."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "The Victorian Institute of Sport Assessment-patellar tendon (VISA-P), visual analogue scale, and Roles and Maudsley were assessed at baseline and at the last follow-up."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "The baseline VISA-P score of 46 ± 18.2 (range, 28-75) improved to 75.3 ± 19.2 (range, 68-100) by 15 months (P = 0.003). The mean pain visual analogue scale changed from 91 mm (range, 66-92 mm) before the injection to 28 mm (2-52 mm) (P = 0.01). Of 32 physically active patients, 23 (72%) had returned to sport at the same level practiced before the onset of symptoms. Thirty-five of the 44 patients (80%) rated their condition as good or excellent."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "High-volume injection at the interface between the deep surface of the patellar tendon and Hoffa body improves in the short-term symptoms and function of the knee."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL RELEVANCE",
          "content": "This procedure is minimally invasive, safe, and effective in the short term in athletes."
        }
      ]},
